# Polymer MC Simulation implementation

Successfully implemented the Monte Carlo parameterization for the given linear polymer as outlined in the requirements.

## 1. Fortran Implementation
A sequential and an OpenMP parallelized version have been built around a modular architecture split among 4 source files (+ 1 precision file):
- [kinds_mod.f90](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/src/kinds_mod.f90): Double precision configuration.
- [polymer_mod.f90](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/src/polymer_mod.f90): Handles polymer $N=500$ topology setup, building Euclidean coordinates from torsions ($1.54$ Å bond lengths, $114^\circ$ bond angles), and computing observables like Radius of Gyration ($R_g$) and End-to-End Distance ($R_{ee}$).
- [energy_mod.f90](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/src/energy_mod.f90): Computes Torsional energies and Lennard-Jones parameters ($\sigma_{LJ} = 3.93$ Å). Here, an OpenMP `!$omp parallel do reduction(+:E)` is invoked in `compute_total_energy` to split the nested $O(N^2)$ calculations across available CPU threads.
- [mc_mod.f90](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/src/mc_mod.f90): Randomly chooses a backbone torsion to perturb within a fixed step size and uses standard Metropolis criteria logic bounded to system $T=300K$ parameterizations.
- [main.f90](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/src/main.f90): Primary loop iterating for 10,000 steps, recording energy statistics dynamically. 

All files are prefixed with their specified authors aligned to the breakdown requests (Person A -> Makefile; Person B -> Core MC logic; Person C -> OpenMP & Energy; Person D -> Plots).

## 2. Make Pipeline
A central [Makefile](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/Makefile) has been added. 
Executing `make all` builds both `poly_mc_serial` and `poly_mc_omp` target executables.
The Makefile features two run commands:
- `make run_serial`: Runs simulation unthreaded.
- `make run_parallel`: Executes binary configured to automatically bind `OMP_NUM_THREADS=4`.
- `make plot`: Triggers python module parsing.

## 3. Results & Plots
The sequential runtime produced `energy.dat` and `trajectory.dat`.
A Python script ([scripts/analyze_polymer.py](file://wsl.localhost/Ubuntu/home/amu/LinuxCoursework/AdvCompTools/GroupProject/scripts/analyze_polymer.py)) handles ingesting Fortran's formatted float logs and uses Matplotlib to compute observables:
- `energy_evolution.png`
- `structural_observables.png`
- `torsion_distribution.png`
